ocratic energy in the 2nd District House race has limited statewide presidential relevance"], "rationale": "Arkansas's deep Republican fundamentals make the GOP nominee a clear favorite, and with no polls, ratings, or market prices available, there is no evidence justifying a move away from the state's partisan lean.", "watch": "The first credible presidential poll in Arkansas, or a major national environment shift toward either party, would be the most likely trigger for revision.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Arkansas remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, and the 2028 race there starts from an R+15 partisan baseline. The incumbent president is term-limited, so no incumbency advantage applies to either nominee. No public polls of the Arkansas presidential race, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available, so expectations rest on the state's partisan fundamentals.\n\nDownballot headlines show some Democratic energy: Chris Jones leads in polling of the Little Rock-based 2nd Congressional District with Kamala Harris's endorsement, and Democrats are contesting legislative specials. But that activity is concentrated in the state's least Republican district and has not produced a competitive statewide presidential picture. The Republican nominee is a clear favorite, with the Democratic path requiring a national environment far more favorable than anything currently indicated.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0496, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0891, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0911, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0924, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0837, "margin": -15.0}